1
00:00:00,362 --> 00:00:02,309
Code can run perfectly

2
00:00:02,491 --> 00:00:04,237
and still get the physics wrong.

3
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:06,481
Start with material properties.

4
00:00:06,821 --> 00:00:08,843
A correct formula still fails

5
00:00:08,983 --> 00:00:10,824
if the constants or units are wrong.

6
00:00:11,144 --> 00:00:12,585
Check the source, units,

7
00:00:12,645 --> 00:00:13,966
and temperature dependence.

8
00:00:15,107 --> 00:00:17,188
Next, check the physical model.

9
00:00:17,789 --> 00:00:19,610
A steady state assumption can miss

10
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:21,711
a process that changes over time.

11
00:00:22,031 --> 00:00:23,512
Choose assumptions that match

12
00:00:23,512 --> 00:00:24,993
the system you want to study.

13
00:00:26,153 --> 00:00:28,314
Then review the boundary conditions.

14
00:00:28,875 --> 00:00:31,296
Walls, sources, constraints,

15
00:00:31,536 --> 00:00:33,877
and initial states define the problem.

16
00:00:34,458 --> 00:00:35,958
If they are inconsistent,

17
00:00:36,118 --> 00:00:37,879
you are solving a different problem.

18
00:00:38,841 --> 00:00:40,964
Now test the numerical setup.

19
00:00:41,184 --> 00:00:44,048
Refine the mesh, check the time step,

20
00:00:44,269 --> 00:00:46,212
and inspect solver tolerances.

21
00:00:46,873 --> 00:00:49,497
A successful run alone does not establish

22
00:00:49,497 --> 00:00:50,839
numerical reliability.

23
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:54,203
Finally, question the interpretation.

24
00:00:54,583 --> 00:00:56,065
A solver can converge

25
00:00:56,145 --> 00:00:58,727
while the model remains physically wrong.

26
00:00:58,948 --> 00:01:01,951
Compare the result with
theory, measurements,

27
00:01:02,071 --> 00:01:03,752
or a suitable benchmark.

28
00:01:04,913 --> 00:01:07,536
AI can accelerate scientific computing.

29
00:01:08,017 --> 00:01:10,339
Scientific expertise still has to

30
00:01:10,339 --> 00:01:13,362
verify the calculation
and validate the model.

31
00:01:13,763 --> 00:01:16,025
Working code is the
beginning of that review.

32
00:01:19,180 --> 00:01:21,403
Scientific work needs more than code.

33
00:01:21,964 --> 00:01:23,686
It needs a checkable workflow.

34
00:01:24,867 --> 00:01:25,948
Start with the method.

35
00:01:26,668 --> 00:01:29,591
A skill document records the steps,
physical

36
00:01:29,591 --> 00:01:31,933
assumptions, and acceptance criteria.

37
00:01:32,513 --> 00:01:35,135
It gives the assistant a reusable
procedure,

38
00:01:35,395 --> 00:01:37,677
so the task follows an explicit scientific

39
00:01:37,677 --> 00:01:40,319
method instead of an improvised answer.

40
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:43,901
Next, connect that method to the tools.

41
00:01:44,081 --> 00:01:46,322
An MCP server gives the assistant a

42
00:01:46,322 --> 00:01:48,743
structured way to call scientific
software.

43
00:01:48,983 --> 00:01:51,984
The physics solver performs the numerical
calculation.

44
00:01:52,385 --> 00:01:54,545
A successful tool call still needs a

45
00:01:54,545 --> 00:01:56,886
correctly defined model and inputs.

46
00:01:58,087 --> 00:02:00,007
Then verify the result.

47
00:02:00,388 --> 00:02:03,829
Check units, physical behavior, and
numerical accuracy

48
00:02:04,129 --> 00:02:06,730
using suitable reference cases and mesh
checks.

49
00:02:07,030 --> 00:02:09,751
Save the inputs, software versions, and
outputs.

50
00:02:10,051 --> 00:02:12,652
These records make the workflow traceable
and

51
00:02:12,652 --> 00:02:15,293
repeatable. They do not guarantee
correctness by

52
00:02:15,293 --> 00:02:15,913
themselves.

53
00:02:17,274 --> 00:02:19,479
Connect the method, tools, and checks.

54
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:21,844
Make the evidence reproducible.

55
00:02:24,008 --> 00:02:25,371
OpenCode has finished.

56
00:02:25,452 --> 00:02:27,096
Let's inspect the evidence

57
00:02:27,096 --> 00:02:28,579
behind the calculation.

58
00:02:29,501 --> 00:02:31,443
After submitting the prepared prompt,

59
00:02:31,784 --> 00:02:34,628
OpenCode organizes the
run into five stages:

60
00:02:34,848 --> 00:02:37,252
connection, model setup, simulation,

61
00:02:37,252 --> 00:02:38,774
validation, and visualization.

62
00:02:39,095 --> 00:02:40,697
Use that structure to inspect

63
00:02:40,697 --> 00:02:42,219
the evidence behind the answer.

64
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:45,721
This example is a ten millimeter steel bar

65
00:02:45,721 --> 00:02:48,083
with a one millimeter
square cross section.

66
00:02:48,523 --> 00:02:49,964
The prescribed extension is

67
00:02:49,964 --> 00:02:51,924
zero point zero one millimeters.

68
00:02:52,345 --> 00:02:54,506
The report lists the material properties,

69
00:02:54,506 --> 00:02:55,926
mesh, and boundary conditions.

70
00:02:56,608 --> 00:02:58,750
There are two complementary runs.

71
00:02:59,031 --> 00:03:00,654
The direct CalculiX benchmark

72
00:03:00,654 --> 00:03:01,955
prescribes the extension.

73
00:03:02,416 --> 00:03:04,439
The MCP cross-check applies

74
00:03:04,439 --> 00:03:05,921
an equivalent axial force.

75
00:03:06,282 --> 00:03:08,005
Keep those loading methods separate

76
00:03:08,005 --> 00:03:09,326
when you compare the results.

77
00:03:09,947 --> 00:03:12,629
The direct test reports consistent results

78
00:03:12,629 --> 00:03:14,190
across three meshes.

79
00:03:14,551 --> 00:03:16,532
For the force-controlled cross-check,

80
00:03:16,653 --> 00:03:18,154
compare midspan stress

81
00:03:18,214 --> 00:03:20,475
and average end displacement with theory.

82
00:03:21,016 --> 00:03:23,538
The report warns that raw maxima include

83
00:03:23,538 --> 00:03:25,720
constraint and load-distribution effects.

84
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:28,422
The validation dashboard brings

85
00:03:28,422 --> 00:03:29,603
those checks together:

86
00:03:29,943 --> 00:03:32,486
axial displacement, lateral contraction,

87
00:03:32,886 --> 00:03:35,128
stress, and comparison with theory.

88
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:37,611
Keep the plot labels and
the loading assumptions

89
00:03:37,611 --> 00:03:39,493
visible as you review the result.

90
00:03:40,374 --> 00:03:43,157
Next, open the simulation folder in Linux.

91
00:03:43,758 --> 00:03:47,221
It contains the input
deck, mesh, solver logs,

92
00:03:47,341 --> 00:03:48,903
extracted results, and plots.

93
00:03:49,383 --> 00:03:51,566
These files let you trace the calculation

94
00:03:51,566 --> 00:03:52,667
and inspect the setup.

95
00:03:53,647 --> 00:03:55,288
Open the input file to review

96
00:03:55,288 --> 00:03:57,229
the material and boundary conditions,

97
00:03:57,469 --> 00:03:59,930
then check the solver log for warnings.

98
00:04:00,391 --> 00:04:02,332
This example is a small strain,

99
00:04:02,332 --> 00:04:03,632
linear elastic check.

100
00:04:03,832 --> 00:04:05,373
Good automation leaves evidence

101
00:04:05,373 --> 00:04:06,073
you can review.
